    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d93">

                <pb facs="0076" n="68" xml:id="pg68"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751G.94/17: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Murphy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vichy</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-08-07T22:00:00+02:00">August 7, 1940—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 7—12:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>278. Chauvel tells me this evening that the Gaimusho<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d93-f1"/> has now taken great pains to explain to Arsene-Henry that
                    the Japanese communication regarding Indochina should by no means be construed
                    as an ultimatum. The Foreign Office is advising Saint-Quentin that the Japanese
                    have expressed the desire to enter into negotiations regarding the matter and
                    have assured the French Government of their wish to avoid a conflict.<anchor
                        n="20" xml:id="d93-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Murphy</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d93-f1" n="19" xml:id="d93-f1-content">
                <p>The Japanese Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d93-f2" n="20" xml:id="d93-f2-content">
                <p>On August 8 the French Counselor of Embassy made similar statements to the Chief
                    of the Division of Far Eastern Affairs (751G.94/41).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
